      "Subjective:\n",
      "The patient, Emily, a 42-year-old female, presents with persistent low back pain of moderate severity lasting over 6 months. She reports the pain to be daily, exacerbated by movement, and impacting her ability to perform daily activities and work. Emily has a prior diagnosis of lumbar disc disease and a family history of fibromyalgia. She denies any known medication allergies. She reports poor sleep quality, a diet high in processed foods, and minimal exercise due to pain. There is no tobacco use, though there is moderate alcohol consumption on weekends, and no illicit drug use. Emily appears motivated to engage in medical advice and is open to non-pharmacological interventions.\n",
      "\n",
      "Objective:\n",
      "General physical examination was unremarkable. Emily's pain is localized to the lumbar region without radiation, and no neurological deficits were detected. Vital signs were within normal limits (specific vital signs measurements would be documented here if available).\n",
      "\n",
      "Assessment:\n",
      "Emily has a confirmed diagnosis of Chronic Pain Syndrome (CPS), which seems to be complex and multifactorial in nature, influenced by her past lumbar disc disease and perhaps a genetic predisposition to fibromyalgia, although the latter is not currently diagnosed.\n",
      "\n",
      "Plan:\n",
      "1. Pharmacological Management: Prescribe Gabapentin, 300 mg oral capsules, three times daily to manage pain with an initial prescription for 30 days and one refill.\n",
      "2. Physical Therapy: Refer Emily to a physiotherapist specializing in pain rehabilitation, for an assessment and a tailored exercise program. Referral to Dr. Maria Gonzalez for physical therapy.\n",
      "3. Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy (CBT): Recommend starting CBT to address the psychological aspects of chronic pain.\n",
      "4. Lifestyle Modifications: Educate Emily on the importance of a balanced diet and the gradual introduction of low-impact exercises as tolerated. Discuss strategies for improving sleep hygiene.\n",
      "5. Follow-Up: Arrange a follow-up appointment for Emily in 4 weeks to assess the response to medication and therapies.\n",
      "6. Investigations: Order a lumbar spine MRI to assess for structural abnormalities contributing to chronic pain and blood tests to rule out inflammatory conditions: CBC, ESR, CRP, RF, and ANA. Plan to follow up on the results when available and adjust the treatment plan accordingly.\n",
      "\n",
      "These recommendations are tailored to Emily's high health literacy, socioeconomic status, and access to recommended services due to geographic location.\n"
     ]
